  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe provided algorithm uses a multi-phase optimization with subgradient descent, coordinate descent (CD), and fine-tuning, but it may be stuck in a local optimum. My approach is to:\n- Use a multi-start strategy with multiple initial conditions\n- Use block initialization for coarse solutions\n- Employ a simulated annealing-like method for better exploration\n- Utilize a targeted CD phase focused on reducing the maximum correlation\n- Balance exploitation with exploration through parallel evolution-inspired population\n\nThis plan aims to overcome the limitations of the current algorithm's local optimization by combining:\n- Explicit repair mechanisms\n- Multi-start optimization\n- Targeted gradient-based search\n- Simpler but effective heuristic methods\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function sampled at `n_points` intervals\n- Minimize: C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h)*dx = 1\n- Objective: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))*(2.0/n_points)\n- Search space: All piecewise constant functions with sum h = 0.5*n_points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm follows these phases:\n\nI. Initialization\n- Multiple initial candidates: Random samples, periodic patterns, and block structures\n- Repair each candidate to satisfy sum constraint and box constraints\n\nII. Subgradient descent (20% budget)\n- Projected gradient method with line search\n- Repair after every iteration to maintain constraints\n\nIII. Coordinated descent (40% budget)\n- Targeted updates - adjust variables based on most-negative gradient\n- Maintain and track the current maximum correlation\n\nIV. Simulated annealing (30% budget)\n- Random walk with temperature schedule\n- Different neighborhoods with varying jump lengths\n- Multi-exponential cooling schedule\n\nV. Block optimization (10% budget)\n- Group coordinates into blocks and optimize macro-derivatives\n- Update entire blocks by respecting sum constraints\n\nVI. Fine polishing (10% budget)\n- Perform local searches in promising directions\n- Use gradient information to focus updates on correlation peaks\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Base temperature scale: 1.0/np.min(c) (initial correlation)\n- Cooling steps: 20-30 cooling steps with cooling factor 0.9-0.99\n- For CD phase: Use gradient to select the worst correlation point\n- For annealing: Try 5-8 random moves per accepted solution\n- For block method: Use exponentially-descending blocks (32-2 block sizes)\n- Multi-starts: 3-5 different initialization methods\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Total budget: 1100s\n- Phase time limits: \n  - Subgradient: 220s\n  - CD: 440s\n  - Annealing: 330s\n  - Block: 110s\n  - Polishing: 50s\n- Temperature restart if blocked: 20% of current temp\n- Repair forced if violated constraints\n- Fallback if stuck: Most recent lowest-correlation solution\n- Maximum iterations: 10,000\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure candidate satisfies 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)=len(h)/2\n- Verify objective calculation consistency\n- Re-run final candidate through objective function\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- n_points=1000 (interpolated from initial 552 sample solution)\n- Other required libraries: numpy, scipy, scipy.optimize.minimize\n\nImplementation details follow:",
